{
 "name": "Iowa MIS Percussion (music-production build)",
 "comment": "Recorded by the University of Iowa Electronic Music Studios (Musical Instrument Samples, https://theremin.music.uiowa.edu/MIS.html) in an anechoic chamber, 5 feet from the instrument; the site states that since 1997 the recordings \"may be downloaded and used for any projects, without restrictions.\" Played by Andrew Thierauf, 2013, stereo, 24-bit/44.1 kHz. Orchestra bells (plastic mallets, chromatic at pp, mf and ff) on program 9, crotales (C6-C8, pp and ff, one file per note) on 10, Thai gongs (C4-C5, pp, mf and ff) on 11, and a kit on bank 128 program 48: clash cymbals, suspended cymbals struck, rolled, choked and bowed, tam-tams of 22, 28 and 40 inches struck and bowed, and a wind gong (keys in references/soundbanks.md). Each take rings until it falls 60 dB under its peak, at most 30 s. Each source file, a chromatic run at one dynamic, is cut into one note per file and every note checked against the run its file is named for (soundbank.py split); then pitch-checked again, intonation offsets within a semitone corrected by fineTune, leading silence trimmed, levels matched per layer with set layer offsets, 16-bit TPDF dither. In build b2 the steady hiss and rumble of the bells, crotales and Thai gongs are reduced by up to 10 dB: a Wiener gain against the noise measured in the quiet frames of each dynamic's takes.",
 "copyright": "Source recordings: University of Iowa Electronic Music Studios, free for any use without restrictions",
 "target_rms": 0.08,
